  "subtype": "success",
  "final_text": "I submitted **part-based** with confidence 0.62. The evidence leans that way but isn't conclusive, because the test results were very noisy. I used the full budget of 60.\n\n**Design**\n1. I trained three tasks that share no rules: L1-S1-M1, L2-S2-M2 and L3-S3-M3. Then I tested tasks with 0, 1, 2 or 3 rules the learner hadn't seen.\n2. I trained three more tasks next to L1-S1-M1: L1-S1-M2, L1-S2-M1 and L2-S1-M1. This made L1-S1-M4 much more similar to the trained set, but it still has one new rule (M4). The two hypotheses disagree here: holistic says it should get much easier, part-based says it should stay as hard as other one-new-rule tasks.\n\n**Results** (average examples needed to reach criterion; lower is easier)\n\n| New rules in the tested task | Average cost |\n|---|---|\n| 0 | about 19 |\n| 1 | about 27 (about 36 before step 2) |\n| 2 | about 28 (only 5 tests) |\n| 3 | about 49, very steady |\n\n- **The key test:** After step 2, L1-S1-M4 averaged about 25 over 10 tests. That's the same as one-new-rule tasks the extra training didn't touch, like L3-S4-M3 (about 30) and L4-S1-M1 (about 26). Its first five tests after step 2 looked dramatically easier (1, 11, 2, 11, 17), but the next five (41, 27, 50, 41, 47) cancelled that out.\n- **Output overlap didn't predict cost:** How often a test task gave the same response as a trained task (the `compare` command) didn't predict cost. For example, L4-S4-M4 matched a trained task on 56% of inputs and was still always the hardest.\n\n**Why confidence isn't higher**\n- Repeating the same test varied by about \u00b115 and sometimes jumped much higher (one test gave 78), so any single comparison is only about two standard errors wide.\n- Tasks with one or two new rules cost about the same, though part-based predicts two should cost more. That rests on only five tests.\n- One-new-rule tasks got somewhat easier overall after step 2, which a strict part-based learner wouldn't predict.\n- A holistic learner whose similarity measure happens to track shared rules would produce the same ordering by new-rule count. Only the L1-S1-M4 test separates the two, and it rests on 10 noisy tests.",
